                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Got to play this game again after many years, and it's stayed surprisingly fresh.\r\n\r\nThanks to its main gimmick, the ability to rotate the whole level along the horizontal axis. \"Hidden\" parts of the scenery with destructible elements, including crates with pick-ups, can be revealed; huge volleys of bullets that would be near impossible to avoid normally become thin streaks; formations of enemies at various heights become straight lines that are much easier to shoot down. Rotation can also reveal many stationary targets for extra point pickups, which also repair a little damage.\r\n\r\nEven without that, it plays great. The self-recharging laser cuts through yellow bullets and is perfect for bigger enemies, and the action is very fast.\r\n\r\nThere are only four levels, the last being just a brief final parade of regular enemies before the final boss; a full run is not expected to last more than 25 minutes. But besides the work required to craft levels around that gimmick, there are some alternate paths. Ultimately, they all loop back to the same places, but they add replay value. The challenge ramps up after the second boss, and there are no selectable difficulty levels or continues. To add some more meat, there are 50 challenges that are intended to also help dedicated players further hone their skills with the game's mechanics.\r\n\r\nThe game is very pretty to look at, and hasn't aged a day since its release, thanks to its visual style; it seems to take place inside some hyper-technological megastructure, or in virtual reality, with the use of almost all blue with streaks of red, save for a few sections where red becomes prevalent. Mecha design is good, bosses look fantastic (the third one has quite the entry), and there's also a great soundtrack to boot, with Level 2's track being my favourite. On top of that, on recent hardware it can be set up to maximum detail with no issues.\r\n\r\nUnfortunately, the same visual style turns against itself, as the action can get hard to read amid all the blue, stylish as it may look. The gameplay may also feel too confusing if you're looking for a more straightforward experience.\r\n\r\nSave for that, honestly, there is little reason not to get [b]REVOLVER360 RE:ACTOR[/b], much less at such a low price (and it goes down to less than €2 when on sale). Its main mechanic has remained unique; there is little similar in video games in general, and nothing like it among shmups.",
